WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.193 --> 00:00:13.630
Today we have Vinod Khosla.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:18.350
A founder of Khosla Ventures and
former founder of Sun Microsystems.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.520
He has advised a lot of
startups in Silicon Valley.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.800
I'm going to interview Vinod today and we
are going to talk about how to build and

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.140
manage teams.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:27.964
Thank you, Vinod.

00:00:27.964 --> 00:00:30.290
&gt;&gt; Thanks [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:36.310
&gt;&gt; So, you've talked a lot about company
building and hiring and I know that you've

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:42.360
often said that a company becomes the
people it hires, not the plan it makes.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.390
So can you elaborate on
what you mean by that?

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.620
And especially for founders who
are just beginning to start a company.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.048
What they should they focus on in hiring?

00:00:52.048 --> 00:00:55.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this one of my favorite topics.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.621
Many, many founders and when you start,

00:01:00.621 --> 00:01:04.829
you know very little
about what you're doing,

00:01:04.829 --> 00:01:09.998
if you did know enough,
you wouldn't start the company.

00:01:09.998 --> 00:01:14.508
If you were really sensible and
pragmatic, you wouldn't start companies.

00:01:14.508 --> 00:01:18.840
I'm an entrepreneurship zealot,
that's self-described, and

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.324
I'm a real fan of people trying
companies and trying to build things,

00:01:23.324 --> 00:01:28.040
especially when you're younger,
when you can afford to take the risks.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:35.790
There's no reason to go work for
somebody else in my book, and I never did.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:44.860
What I find is people have plans,

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:50.120
but it is very, very rare for
a company to achieve that plan.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:57.338
So the way to think about where
you're starting is a collection

00:01:57.338 --> 00:02:05.080
of ideas in a relatively rich space that
you find that you can be passionate about.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.171
That's the way to think about a plan.

00:02:08.171 --> 00:02:15.117
I have a separate talk on flexi-planning,
which is don't plan, just plan to plan.

00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:20.243
But the critical part of that is
the team you build ends up making

00:02:20.243 --> 00:02:25.480
all day derivative decisions about
where you're going to end up.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:32.750
Whether to hire the next person from this
area or that area with that strength or

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.960
some other strength or to take path a or b

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.060
is very,
very critical to where a company ends up.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.280
There's a huge path dependence,
and the path depends so

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.740
much on the people you hire.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:55.640
Your starting plan becomes much less
relevant than the people you hired

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.728
initially, especially
the first ten people.

00:03:03.151 --> 00:03:07.532
Those 10 people will then
go on to hire the next 50,

00:03:07.532 --> 00:03:10.788
and they will then hire the last 100.

00:03:10.788 --> 00:03:16.690
You can fire the original team, and
it's gene DNA stays in the company.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.926
So that's why it's so important, and

00:03:20.926 --> 00:03:24.746
why the people you hire
determine the company becomes.

00:03:24.746 --> 00:03:26.402
And we can come back later and

00:03:26.402 --> 00:03:30.130
talk about where I see mistakes
made around functional hiring.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:36.010
People always ask me and I say,
this gets tweeted a lot,

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:42.150
there's a huge difference between a $0
million company, and a $0 billion company.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.660
And the difference in attitude and
ambition, and

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:50.660
mostly the kind of team you build,
which will reinforce certain

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:55.240
directions for
you especially who else you end up hiring.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.360
So it's far more critical
than people believe.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.700
And it's really hard to
hire those skills later.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:11.777
So hence, my-
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I think you touched upon

00:04:11.777 --> 00:04:17.012
the first time hire being so important and
especially defining pretty much.

00:04:17.012 --> 00:04:20.697
Even the cultural, the belief system
that he would call for the company.

00:04:20.697 --> 00:04:25.713
So if a lot of founders here have
like a one or two member teams,

00:04:25.713 --> 00:04:31.806
how should they think about you know,
who should be those first few hires?

00:04:31.806 --> 00:04:35.270
And how do they go about
finding those candidates?

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.361
And I know you also have talked, you have
a strong view about how equity should be

00:04:39.361 --> 00:04:42.305
split for
the first 10 employees versus the next 30.

00:04:42.305 --> 00:04:45.344
So, I would love to hear how
should a founder think about that?

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:54.419
&gt;&gt; So many start ups are very tactical and
they should be.

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:59.790
But in doing the tactics you
can sometimes forget what

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:06.420
the big vision is and
you may even exclude the big vision.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:10.810
So I was talking to a company's
very recently in our portfolio, and

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:15.490
they had hired a bunch of people to do
particular tasks that needed doing.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:22.040
And there was a new higher,
potentially a big, higher,

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.400
very attractive person
to have join the team.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.200
And I asked the founder,
who's going to interview him?

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.206
And he told me who would
interview this founder?

00:05:33.206 --> 00:05:35.686
And I said if I was a really
great candidate and

00:05:35.686 --> 00:05:40.460
this three people interviewed me first, I
would drop out of that recruiting process.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:46.185
So I said, you know what why
don't I be the first interviewer?

00:05:46.185 --> 00:05:49.000
So that it can be a really
waste of my time.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:55.015
because I want him to grab the big vision
first, and not just be interviewed.

00:05:55.015 --> 00:06:00.620
Let me sell him on the big idea, and
why he can be a part of forming it.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:05.240
And so, the best people
almost can't be interviewed.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.740
They have to be sold and evaluated.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:09.970
Evaluation is important,

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:14.560
but evaluation and interviewing
are different things in my mind.

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:21.415
So once you hired the first five, and
they're interviewing other people,

00:06:21.415 --> 00:06:24.825
you might actually exclude
the best candidates.

00:06:24.825 --> 00:06:27.800
And that's very,
very tricky for a founder.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:33.181
At the same time, sometimes you
can't afford the big names, so

00:06:33.181 --> 00:06:35.660
you don't have enough to attract them.

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:36.690
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:38.916
&gt;&gt; And those are very tricky times and

00:06:38.916 --> 00:06:42.980
that's why I think whether
you're a $0 million company, or

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:47.985
a $0 billion company sort of gets set
by the time you're 15 or 20 people.

00:06:47.985 --> 00:06:48.485
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:53.600
&gt;&gt; That people who've done it very poorly,
I won’t give you examples.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.620
But people like Strike Patrick
did it amazingly

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:02.458
great job of his early hires and
has continued to do that.

00:07:02.458 --> 00:07:02.994
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:09.120
&gt;&gt; So you can completely change the
trajectory of a company by that hiring,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.830
and how big you'll become.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.220
&gt;&gt; Now what are some of the things
that you think Patrick at Strike or

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.580
some of these successful founders
have done in the first year.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:21.950
Is it that they meet
candidates in other roles?

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:25.140
Because the founder also often doesn't
know a lot of the the other roles.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:29.780
Or doesn't have domain knowledge about
the roles that they are looking to hire.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:34.265
&gt;&gt; So let's talk about each of those
separately because they're quite

00:07:34.265 --> 00:07:35.508
distinct things.

00:07:35.508 --> 00:07:43.740
First, I think of company building
as going after Mount Everest.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:47.352
But nobody got to Mount Everest
without getting to Basecamp first.

00:07:50.828 --> 00:07:52.941
Here's the misstep.

00:07:52.941 --> 00:07:54.940
You can't get to Everest without Basecamp.

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:59.749
And many people forget that
Basecamp has to lead to Everest.

00:07:59.749 --> 00:08:03.890
So they set up Basecamp where it doesn't
help reduce the risk of Everest.

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:10.260
And so building a company, you never
shoot for the big enchilada in one step.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:15.660
You go after base camp one, then base
camp two, it's a very good analogy.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:23.562
However, especially with bad investors,
and I have a lot of beefs about investors,

00:08:23.562 --> 00:08:28.921
they want revenue or they want break-even,
or they want management.

00:08:28.921 --> 00:08:33.659
All of the things that can to lead to
setting up business here that then

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:39.461
excludes the possibility that you'll learn
more about how to get to base camp two and

00:08:39.461 --> 00:08:43.401
three, you are out in some
other local minima or maxima.

00:08:43.401 --> 00:08:46.909
And that's really dangerous early on, too.

00:08:46.909 --> 00:08:51.782
In fact, one of the beefs,
I was just talking to somebody in

00:08:51.782 --> 00:08:55.497
the YC class and
started talking about that.

00:08:55.497 --> 00:09:03.488
I said, and
this is one of my pieces of advice to YC.

00:09:03.488 --> 00:09:07.455
If you get too tactical and
forget the strategic,

00:09:07.455 --> 00:09:10.580
you set up base camp in the wrong place.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:14.891
You want to decide which is the right
space you want to go after with

00:09:14.891 --> 00:09:17.290
the technology in this case.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:22.330
Starting with a great technology before
you start doing all of the tactical stuff.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:27.490
So there's this uncomfortable balance
between tactics and strategy,

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:32.831
I'd say be obstinate about your vision,
be flexible about your tactics.

00:09:32.831 --> 00:09:36.540
Another way I'd like to say it
is think big but act small.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:41.150
These are really difficult things to
navigate, much easier said than done.

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:43.873
But back to this issue.

00:09:46.959 --> 00:09:50.850
Let me talk about a process I
call gene pool engineering.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:57.842
If you're going after a space,
assuming you've picked the right space,

00:09:57.842 --> 00:10:00.871
or the one you're passionate about,

00:10:00.871 --> 00:10:05.903
it doesn't even have to be right farther,
just right for you.

00:10:05.903 --> 00:10:10.050
The first thing I suggest people do is
see all the ways in which you can fail.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:12.720
Think about it.

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:20.109
You want to identify the key risks
in whatever you're trying to do.

00:10:20.109 --> 00:10:24.372
Then you hire for those risks.

00:10:24.372 --> 00:10:27.630
So I always say to people,
find your top five risks.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:32.375
For each of those risks, say risk one,

00:10:32.375 --> 00:10:36.759
who are the companies that
have dealt with that risk?

00:10:36.759 --> 00:10:39.973
Where does the expertise on that risk lie?

00:10:39.973 --> 00:10:41.883
Make a list of three to five companies.

00:10:41.883 --> 00:10:46.000
And by the way, there's document you'll
get a link called Gene Pool Engineering.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.410
That's on our website that I
wrote just for this purpose.

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:57.340
If you've identified the five companies
that have dealt with that risk,

00:10:57.340 --> 00:11:01.959
then you have at least the places
that can reduce your risk.

00:11:01.959 --> 00:11:05.036
Within each company, identify three names.

00:11:05.036 --> 00:11:09.570
Now, you have 15 names for
the risk number 1.

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:15.135
If you do that for your top five risks,
you essentially have

00:11:15.135 --> 00:11:21.723
a library of names that will help you
reduce the risk in your business.

00:11:21.723 --> 00:11:24.520
And that's risk-oriented management.

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:25.660
And startups,

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:31.460
after you decide what space you're going
after is all about risk management.

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:33.190
There's lots of risk tradeoffs.

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.930
Every time you hire somebody,
you increase your burden.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:40.867
You're taking on financial risk, but
you're reducing some other risk.

00:11:40.867 --> 00:11:44.090
Maybe it's product risk or
development risk or feature risk.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.491
So it's a series of tradeoffs.

00:11:46.491 --> 00:11:52.100
That's how startups should think
about what they're trying to do.

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:55.880
So this a process I call
Gene Pool Engineering.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:12:00.700
Where you engineer 15 names for
each of your major risks, and

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:05.130
then go after all of them, and
hopefully hire 1 or 2 of the 15.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:09.370
And so you've then engineered
the gene pool of your company for

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:12.290
the risks that are in the plan
you're going to pursue.

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:17.710
It's way more precise than the platitude
of let's hire great people.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:23.550
I find that really disturbing because
I've never actually had a startup

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:27.070
coming to me and
say that they're only hiring bad people.

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:30.758
Never quite happens.

00:12:30.758 --> 00:12:35.710
So how do you make platitudes into
something concrete and actionable.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.210
That's a big thing with me.

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:44.144
The second part of that is
the reality that startups

00:12:44.144 --> 00:12:49.670
seldom pursue the original
plan they started.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:52.163
So they've hired.

00:12:52.163 --> 00:12:56.091
You also have to hire not only for
the risks you know but

00:12:56.091 --> 00:13:02.025
all the other things that you might pivot
to, change to, adapt to, iterate to,

00:13:02.025 --> 00:13:07.110
because building a business plan in
a new area is a series of federations.

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:14.100
So suddenly, this risk requirement goes
in parallel with this other requirement,

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:17.640
which I call smart adaptation.

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:22.645
You adapt very quickly to whatever
the market is teaching you.

00:13:22.645 --> 00:13:28.340
YC is particularly good at that, teaching
you how to iterate quickly and learn,

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:33.270
and essentially, very similar to
what I call this flexi-planning

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:38.650
process where you iterate your way to
a plan instead of saying this is my plan.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:44.920
I always worry when people
make plans seven years out and

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:52.081
give me forecasts because it tells me they
don't have a clue what they don't know.

00:13:52.081 --> 00:13:55.934
And that is one of the great benefits
of YC, from my point of view,

00:13:55.934 --> 00:13:57.697
this integrative approach.

00:13:57.697 --> 00:14:03.420
Other people call it MVP, or there's
other names for this iterative approach.

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:05.610
I call it flexi-planning.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:12.240
But the team you built, in addition
to this risk approach, also has to be

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:17.878
open to new ideas, grab new ideas,
ask a lot of questions, adapt quickly,

00:14:17.878 --> 00:14:23.670
brainstorm with others constructively,
so you build on each other's ideas.

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:27.670
These are our parallel set of
requirements around hiring

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.660
that are very hard to quantify.

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:34.883
They're also full of danger.

00:14:34.883 --> 00:14:37.120
They're full of danger for
the following reasons.

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:41.000
Sometimes people don't hire for this.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.995
That's a danger.

00:14:42.995 --> 00:14:47.675
At other times, and one of the biggest
risks if you decide you want to be

00:14:47.675 --> 00:14:52.614
aggressive about hiring is going after
names or titles or other things.

00:14:52.614 --> 00:14:56.226
Big names and
titles in big companies are really,

00:14:56.226 --> 00:14:59.761
really dangerous and
misleading for startups.

00:14:59.761 --> 00:15:04.790
My biggest mistakes early on
in my career was getting too

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:10.361
impressed with somebody's title at IBM or
some big company.

00:15:10.361 --> 00:15:14.310
Now, I take the view, if somebody has
been at a big company for 10 or 15 years.

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:18.968
If they've been at Cisco for ten years,
they can't work effectively in a startup,

00:15:18.968 --> 00:15:23.390
because they'll get re-brainwashed by
failing once or twice in the startup.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:25.280
Then it'll work.

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:30.287
So hiring for this other stuff and

00:15:30.287 --> 00:15:36.306
not falling into the trap of just
big names is very important.

00:15:36.306 --> 00:15:38.587
It also depends upon your domain.

00:15:38.587 --> 00:15:43.619
If the domain your startup is in is

00:15:43.619 --> 00:15:50.465
radically different than than your actual.

00:15:50.465 --> 00:15:51.822
Let me put it differently.

00:15:51.822 --> 00:15:58.131
Some domains need a lot of expertise.

00:15:58.131 --> 00:16:01.240
So if you're doing a broadcast like you
can drop out of school and start that.

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:05.730
But eventually, it's a serious physics and
chemistry problem.

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:09.120
And you have to get that Callington boat.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:14.310
If you're doing something
like stripe death.

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:16.190
It was a very simple problem.

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:17.770
You don't need three PhDs.

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:25.160
If you're going to solve healthcare
with AI, you're going to need expertise.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:26.610
So it depends on the domain.

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:28.249
You don't always need the gurus.

00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:30.520
It depends on your problem.

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:33.245
You don't always need the big names.

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:39.163
Another very counterintuitive thing,
we were recruiting for

00:16:39.163 --> 00:16:41.426
a CEO for our company, Live Corp.

00:16:44.377 --> 00:16:49.150
They essentially do cardiac care and
I insisted I would not talk to any

00:16:49.150 --> 00:16:52.370
candidate who would ever
work in healthcare.

00:16:54.230 --> 00:17:00.770
And that's because healthcare
executives tend to apply old rules,

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:03.090
because their domain
hasn't changed very much.

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:09.010
People are so used to applying old
rules and applying good process.

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:14.620
As oppose doing inventing this new future,
this new path.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:17.980
And that's very dangerous.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:22.110
We should come back and
talk about how founders

00:17:22.110 --> 00:17:26.240
figure all this out when they don't have
a clue what these other functions are.

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:33.110
That's really dangerous and
so that's a big, big caution.

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:38.520
It's seldom that a person out of
a domain can integrate in a domain.

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:43.120
If you are a young founder and

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:48.280
you hire somebody with deeper
experience in the domain, it is logical

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:52.650
as human beings to defer more to their
knowledge and that is a real problem.

00:17:53.930 --> 00:17:57.220
It's important to understand
the problems they raise.

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:00.420
Not the solutions they propose.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:02.880
Because if you take that
you keep doing what

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:06.430
traditional people in
that industry have done.

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:11.160
This is why CEO hired into
companies don't work very well and

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:14.690
why founder vision becomes so
important and so critical.

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:19.710
I'm wandering all over the place,
but correct me.

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:21.708
&gt;&gt; No this is how, I think you've also,

00:18:21.708 --> 00:18:25.837
before we touch on the functional hiring
and how to look for domain knowledge.

00:18:25.837 --> 00:18:30.100
I wanted to touch upon one other element
that you talked about in the gene

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:34.520
pool hiding people which is about
diversity and not in the literal sense.

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:37.854
But he talked about there are different
dimensions of diversity that

00:18:37.854 --> 00:18:40.796
are important, as you are trying
to figure out candidates.

00:18:40.796 --> 00:18:44.848
Can you elaborate on like what are like
different dimensions of diversity

00:18:44.848 --> 00:18:48.781
the start ups should focus on and
why is it important to do that early on?

00:18:48.781 --> 00:18:52.461
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so if you buy the pieces

00:18:54.398 --> 00:18:59.320
That most of what you end up executing
on isn't part of your plans today, then

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:04.560
the single most important thing becomes
people who can help you evolve your plan.

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:12.590
And the more diverse the set of people and
I don't mean diverse, ethnically.

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:17.838
I mean, backgrounds and
places they've worked,

00:19:17.838 --> 00:19:22.607
areas they know, age, level of experience.

00:19:22.607 --> 00:19:25.080
There's some really great
things about experience.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:27.060
You want experience.

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:32.980
But you don't want experience to have you,
guide you into

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:36.420
doing what others have already done in
that same business and not innovate.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:39.670
That is the result of too much experience.

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:47.690
So mixing the right experience,
where you can identify the problems,

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:49.610
with first principles thinking.

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:56.072
From fresh new ideas people who
never worked in the domain or

00:19:56.072 --> 00:20:00.957
people who actually are just fresh
graduates and have no idea but

00:20:00.957 --> 00:20:06.316
can ask fundamental questions,
those are the best kind of founders.

00:20:06.316 --> 00:20:09.720
Put it differently, in big companies
you need people with good process.

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:17.546
In start up you need people with good
iteration and good adaptation and

00:20:17.546 --> 00:20:23.334
those a very different skills because
a startup you invest 90% of staff

00:20:23.334 --> 00:20:29.518
in big companies 90% of what you do this
year will be what was done last year.

00:20:29.518 --> 00:20:33.480
And so there's very little innovation
takes a different kind of person.

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:37.870
Most valuable, in fact every startup,
if they are successful,

00:20:37.870 --> 00:20:40.660
will need some process people later.

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:44.510
But process people early in
a startup can be a disaster.

00:20:46.330 --> 00:20:49.310
Too good a manager is a bad thing for
early startups.

00:20:50.930 --> 00:20:54.280
But at some point,
you'll really need to add that.

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:57.327
So that's what I mean by diversity.

00:20:57.327 --> 00:21:02.350
If you have a bunch of PhDs, how many are
experienced PhDs and how many are fresh?

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:06.640
How many are from within your area and how
many are from completely outside the area?

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:13.900
Those, every dimension
of diversity will help

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:16.680
because they'll bring
a different point of view.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:21.740
As long as they have this ability
to think from first principles,

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:26.030
not say, this is done this way
because we did it this way before.

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:36.760
I should also, should probably
say this fied's way more complex,

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:40.990
this is why I've written about
it extensively in two articles.

00:21:40.990 --> 00:21:43.109
One's called the Art and
Science of Hiring.

00:21:43.109 --> 00:21:44.010
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:47.809
You'll get a link to that,
it's on our website too,

00:21:47.809 --> 00:21:50.432
once called gene pool engineering.

00:21:50.432 --> 00:21:54.675
There is a third one that I actually
wrote when Katie Boyd joined us.

00:21:54.675 --> 00:21:58.690
We talks about what board should and
shouldn't do.

00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:03.460
Boards do more damage to start
up than anything else I know.

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:07.636
And that's why I don't go
to board meetings anymore.

00:22:07.636 --> 00:22:09.997
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:22:15.033 --> 00:22:17.808
&gt;&gt; Applying tradition just really hurts,

00:22:17.808 --> 00:22:21.889
so when you're looking for
an investor, it's not money,

00:22:21.889 --> 00:22:26.150
it's help in building the steam
that's way more important.

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:32.946
Way, way more important to where
you get to, than anything around

00:22:32.946 --> 00:22:38.160
the amount of money raised or
the valuation, or other things.

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:43.588
We should come back and
talk about founder equity, also.

00:22:43.588 --> 00:22:49.077
But I spend a third of my time,
maybe more time on recruiting for

00:22:49.077 --> 00:22:53.236
our companies than any
other single thing I do.

00:22:53.236 --> 00:22:54.650
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:57.770
&gt;&gt; Because of all the things
I've talked about.

00:22:57.770 --> 00:22:59.860
In fact, at start up grind,

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:07.190
the interviewer asked me, he said Chamat

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:12.320
was saying that you couldn't possibly
spend that much time interviewing.

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:14.230
And I said that's because

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:20.060
he just fundamentally has
a different belief system than me.

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:20.850
I do.

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:22.900
And it's most of what I do.

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:28.190
I'll go through a 100 LinkedIn
resumes by myself on a weekend.

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:30.780
If I'm looking for
a critical hire for a company.

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:34.680
because a few people can make or
break a company.

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:38.790
&gt;&gt; Yeah I think you've talked about
this too, which would be helpful here.

00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:43.088
Like the CEO's job initially is to be
the chief recruiting officer, right?

00:23:43.088 --> 00:23:43.660
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:48.110
can you elaborate on what is the founder's
role, and how should they be thinking.

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:51.170
Especially because we see a lot
of startups where they would say,

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:54.180
I'm looking for VPF Sales and
they would write down all the skill.

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:57.310
But then, they forget about
all of the elements needed for

00:23:57.310 --> 00:24:00.020
what it takes to lead in a startup.

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:03.400
&gt;&gt; Yes, let's come back to this
notion of function hiring.

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:06.890
Let me talk about what
founders need to get.

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:09.440
A founders role.

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:15.605
And when I was a founder, I spent
probably 40% of my time recruiting.

00:24:15.605 --> 00:24:17.640
It's not coding.

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:23.040
You're much better off hiring
the people who can code than coding.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:27.030
&gt;&gt; And was it throughout the life
of the startup or did it change?

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:30.910
&gt;&gt; Both in the beginning and
throughout the life of the startup.

00:24:30.910 --> 00:24:35.906
I think it's true of very early mid
stage and later stage companies,

00:24:35.906 --> 00:24:38.929
and really good founders do most of that.

00:24:38.929 --> 00:24:42.667
That if you can hire the people,
you should generally

00:24:42.667 --> 00:24:48.440
be able to hire the people who can do
any given job better than you can.

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:50.750
And I tell founders about their role.

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:55.755
If there's any job you can hire for
modular money which we can talk about.

00:24:55.755 --> 00:24:59.445
&gt;&gt; Hm-mm
&gt;&gt; You should hire for that role,

00:24:59.445 --> 00:25:01.525
so you don't have to spend time.

00:25:01.525 --> 00:25:04.910
Founders have some unique characteristics.

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:09.242
They have sort of this entrepreneurial
spirit, this first principle,

00:25:09.242 --> 00:25:10.240
this thinking.

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:13.991
This attitude says, yeah,
it's done this way, but why?

00:25:13.991 --> 00:25:21.510
This why, and I recommend a book by
Simon Sinek called Start With Why.

00:25:21.510 --> 00:25:26.670
It's a really good book about
questioning everything.

00:25:29.475 --> 00:25:35.325
And yet staying true to your vision,
even if it's not precise.

00:25:35.325 --> 00:25:39.455
If you're doing something radically new,
it's going to be ambiguous.

00:25:41.035 --> 00:25:47.540
And very, very few normal people
are comfortable with that ambiguity.

00:25:47.540 --> 00:25:52.135
Let's charge ahead in this
direction even though we don't know

00:25:52.135 --> 00:25:56.727
precisely what the business model is or
why or what it will take,

00:25:56.727 --> 00:25:59.550
that leadership is a founder's role.

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:04.490
And that's something it's
almost impossible to hire for.

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:08.780
If you hire a senior executive
from another company,

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:11.050
there is zero chance you'll have that.

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:14.800
They can systematize what you're doing and

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:20.483
get much more boost to your efforts,
but not without your vision.

00:26:20.483 --> 00:26:25.310
And so that's a founder's role,
I call it leadership.

00:26:25.310 --> 00:26:29.010
It's belief system in
the face of ambiguity.

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:31.990
It's figuring it out kind of attitude.

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:34.380
Assembling a diverse team.

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:36.450
I was very lucky as a young founder.

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:38.980
I loved awesome people.

00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:43.940
I didn't know why we
hired many of the people.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:48.771
But if you look at it, the first
15 people we hired at Sun probably

00:26:48.771 --> 00:26:53.888
started more than 10 companies
worth a billion dollars, later on.

00:26:53.888 --> 00:26:57.250
We hired Carol Bartz who
became CEO of Yahoo.

00:26:57.250 --> 00:27:02.401
We hired Erick Schmidt who became
CEO of Google, we hired all these.

00:27:02.401 --> 00:27:08.007
We hired Bill Joy, in fact Bill Joy became
a founder six months after we started,

00:27:08.007 --> 00:27:09.830
we should come back to it.

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:15.761
I so badly wanted him,
we made him founder retroactively.

00:27:15.761 --> 00:27:19.607
Anything is okay in this game
of assembling great talent.

00:27:19.607 --> 00:27:21.190
&gt;&gt; Mm-mm.

00:27:21.190 --> 00:27:25.340
&gt;&gt; I loved learning from great people,
so we hired great people.

00:27:25.340 --> 00:27:29.680
&gt;&gt; In retrospect,
that was why we were successful.

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:32.889
And I started believing the company
becomes the people you hire.

00:27:32.889 --> 00:27:37.377
By the way, Andy Bechtolsheim was
a student in Magaret Jacks Hall,

00:27:37.377 --> 00:27:42.101
not far from here, where the computer
science department used to be and

00:27:42.101 --> 00:27:45.430
he'd licensed his technology
to six companies.

00:27:46.760 --> 00:27:51.740
The only one that succeeded
with it is us and we started

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:56.090
two years after some of the other
companies had licensed the technology.

00:27:56.090 --> 00:27:58.115
But we got all the people.

00:27:58.115 --> 00:27:59.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:27:59.210 --> 00:28:03.600
&gt;&gt; I hounded Andy till he
dropped his PhD to join me.

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:07.096
I hounded Bill Joy till he
dropped his PhD to join us.

00:28:07.096 --> 00:28:11.247
So both of them dropped their PhDs
when they thought they were six months

00:28:11.247 --> 00:28:13.110
from finishing.

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:14.489
Selling is also a founder's job.

00:28:14.489 --> 00:28:18.956
And it's gut-wrenching because you don't
know you're going to be successful and

00:28:18.956 --> 00:28:22.009
you're trying to convince
somebody to drop their PhD.

00:28:22.009 --> 00:28:25.797
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's gut-wrenching,

00:28:25.797 --> 00:28:28.422
hiring is gut-wrenching.

00:28:28.422 --> 00:28:32.358
Or you're convincing them to drop
a comfortable job at Google and join you?

00:28:35.351 --> 00:28:38.135
So many stories.

00:28:38.135 --> 00:28:41.495
Anyway, back to what
were we talking about.

00:28:41.495 --> 00:28:46.475
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no, I think just to, before
you have said in one of the points that

00:28:46.475 --> 00:28:49.675
when it comes to selling for
hiring, no means maybe.

00:28:49.675 --> 00:28:51.115
And then maybe means yes.

00:28:51.115 --> 00:28:52.265
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I have my favorite.

00:28:52.265 --> 00:28:55.820
When a candidate says no,
I convince them it's a maybe.

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:57.280
Then I'll spend enough time.

00:28:57.280 --> 00:29:01.000
Once they say maybe,
then my job is to say maybe is a yes.

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:04.230
So, I always say a no is a maybe,
and a maybe is a yes.

00:29:05.230 --> 00:29:08.900
And it doesn't matter if you fail.

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:10.819
Occasionally when you succeed,
it's worth it.

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:16.770
People give up on recruiting way
too early and way too quickly.

00:29:16.770 --> 00:29:18.422
I just hound people.

00:29:18.422 --> 00:29:22.566
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I wanted to touch upon founder's equity

00:29:22.566 --> 00:29:24.810
because I know you brought that up twice.

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:29.212
So how should,
especially when you're trying to attract,

00:29:29.212 --> 00:29:31.900
you want to build a great team right?

00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:36.530
And a great team is a magnet for
other people to join the company.

00:29:36.530 --> 00:29:42.318
How should founders think about
equity especially in the early hires?

00:29:42.318 --> 00:29:44.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:49.570
So, I find by and large and
there are exceptions, plenty of them.

00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:53.160
Founders keep too much equity for
themselves.

00:29:55.150 --> 00:29:58.860
And they don't realize the following fact,

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:03.960
anybody they hired whose exceptional
will a year later figure out

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:06.857
they want to start their own company
unless they are a founder, right?

00:30:08.590 --> 00:30:10.160
So if somebody's really, so

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:14.610
you self select for okay people,
good people, not awesome people.

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:20.470
So my son's starting a company,
actually two companies right now.

00:30:20.470 --> 00:30:22.440
He graduated last June from Stanford.

00:30:23.600 --> 00:30:30.265
And I convinced him that him and
his co-founder should just take 15% each,

00:30:30.265 --> 00:30:36.480
leave 15% for a third founder, and
another 15% for three junior founders.

00:30:39.250 --> 00:30:42.390
And then, a 20% pool after that.

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:44.180
So they have this humongous pool.

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:50.470
I said, because I fundamentally believe
a company becomes the people it hires.

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:56.270
I said,
you will be able to hire great people.

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:57.800
So, for one of his companies and

00:30:57.800 --> 00:31:01.150
I think this is public,
if it's not, stop recording.

00:31:02.560 --> 00:31:06.650
He just hired, for one of his companies,
the VP of engineering of Quora.

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:13.640
Why would that person
join a fresh graduate?

00:31:13.640 --> 00:31:18.330
Because, one, he helped define
a fairly interesting large vision.

00:31:18.330 --> 00:31:23.600
But two, he had this huge book and
he's been talking to

00:31:23.600 --> 00:31:27.879
people who would take that kind of thing,
any of whom could start their own company.

00:31:29.270 --> 00:31:34.025
Let me invert it and say,
when a team comes in to present to me,

00:31:34.025 --> 00:31:38.791
one of the questions I ask myself
in judging how good a team is.

00:31:38.791 --> 00:31:42.800
Yeah, do they have expertise in
all the areas they need expertise.

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:48.890
But the single most important question
I ask, on this team, if some of

00:31:48.890 --> 00:31:53.480
these people left and started a company,
would I want to invest in that company?

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:57.800
If somebody has two people
like that on their team,

00:31:57.800 --> 00:31:59.400
zero chance I won't want to invest.

00:32:01.210 --> 00:32:02.800
Independent of whether I like the plan or

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:05.920
not, because they
are collecting great talent.

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:12.010
If I could have great talent or great
a plan, I'd rather have great talent and

00:32:12.010 --> 00:32:14.440
somebody, a founder who
loves great talent.

00:32:15.560 --> 00:32:20.246
So, it's a great way to judge a start up
is, what kind of people do they hire?

00:32:20.246 --> 00:32:27.414
I was telling anyway,
I told Sam in the last YC batch.

00:32:27.414 --> 00:32:31.624
There was two companies we made
an offer to if they hired,

00:32:31.624 --> 00:32:35.059
in a critical area,
they bought AI startups.

00:32:35.059 --> 00:32:40.010
If they left cofounder equity,
equal cofounder equity for an AI startup.

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:45.350
I said, I'll write you a check at
the valuation you want if you do that

00:32:45.350 --> 00:32:48.170
if not, then we don't want to invest.

00:32:48.170 --> 00:32:52.991
One of them took our offer and
the other one didn't, which is okay.

00:32:52.991 --> 00:32:58.357
But I really religiously believed this
idea you get a build joy already.

00:32:58.357 --> 00:32:59.113
And they want experience.

00:32:59.113 --> 00:33:00.534
They were graduates.

00:33:00.534 --> 00:33:02.750
But they made the whole company.

00:33:02.750 --> 00:33:04.639
Eric Schmidt wanted to come work for them.

00:33:05.960 --> 00:33:10.390
By the time we had first ten people,
we were getting so

00:33:10.390 --> 00:33:13.020
many resumes, everybody wanted to join us.

00:33:14.180 --> 00:33:19.341
Because we had so many awesome people
other than, I was sort of, me and

00:33:19.341 --> 00:33:24.691
McNealy were just fresh business
school graduates, we didn't count.

00:33:24.691 --> 00:33:27.520
I was saying and I keep telling my son.

00:33:27.520 --> 00:33:29.164
Hire some magnets.

00:33:29.164 --> 00:33:33.836
But people, I tell him,
you graduate from Standford, you're young,

00:33:33.836 --> 00:33:37.140
nobody is going to come join
you just to work with you.

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:43.690
Think about who you could get that
everybody wants to work with,

00:33:43.690 --> 00:33:51.530
that's how you'll hire
numbers ten through 30 or 50.

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:56.350
A rough rule of thumb I use on equity,
I think Sam has said at least

00:33:56.350 --> 00:34:00.530
10% of the company should be reserved for
the first ten hires.

00:34:00.530 --> 00:34:03.640
I actually think that number is too low.

00:34:04.940 --> 00:34:08.015
I flipped around, saving a company's 50.

00:34:08.015 --> 00:34:12.020
The founders should have
a third of the common.

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:16.040
Their direct reports should have
served as, five or six direct reports,

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:17.110
should have a third of the common.

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:19.370
And everybody else,

00:34:19.370 --> 00:34:23.709
the engineers, the workers bees,
should have a third of the common.

00:34:25.180 --> 00:34:27.920
If you do that, people don't leave.

00:34:27.920 --> 00:34:29.204
People stay with you.

00:34:29.204 --> 00:34:33.600
Now, Sam has a good blackboard
stands how to attract the team.

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:36.090
One of those is growth.

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:39.720
Growth is always a metric but
you can't always close that.

00:34:39.720 --> 00:34:43.860
When things get tough, when you run
into trouble, why will people stay?

00:34:44.990 --> 00:34:48.090
They'll stay because they
believe in your value system.

00:34:48.090 --> 00:34:51.110
What are you going after
the vision you're defining?

00:34:51.110 --> 00:34:55.090
Define an interesting and
ambitious enough vision.

00:34:55.090 --> 00:34:58.620
If your vision is just
to make money the people

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:02.730
the first dip will say I joined to make
money doesn't look like it, they'll leave.

00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:06.870
They won't stick with it.

00:35:06.870 --> 00:35:11.810
But the single biggest reason people
stick with a company is because they have

00:35:11.810 --> 00:35:16.294
people around them who are really awesome,
that they can learn from,

00:35:16.294 --> 00:35:17.980
who are better than them.

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:19.425
Everybody should feel that way.

00:35:19.425 --> 00:35:20.000
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:25.780
&gt;&gt; That's a great culture to set up and
every time I've seen that done,

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:30.900
it's turned out really well.

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:33.330
The gene pool engineering
article I mentioned,

00:35:34.360 --> 00:35:38.430
I put together in 1995,
when we were starting Juniper.

00:35:39.730 --> 00:35:44.870
Nobody had ever challenged Cisco and
nobody wanted to challenge Cisco.

00:35:44.870 --> 00:35:51.528
And no customer in the world
wanted to by TCP/IP routers.

00:35:51.528 --> 00:35:53.760
None, for the public network.

00:35:53.760 --> 00:35:58.421
It sounds incredulous, but
go look up articles from 1995.

00:35:58.421 --> 00:36:04.285
Not a single carrier,
AT&amp;T which was Pac Bell,

00:36:04.285 --> 00:36:07.410
which is now Verizon.

00:36:07.410 --> 00:36:11.930
All said they would never introduce
this PIP in the public network.

00:36:11.930 --> 00:36:14.550
And we defined this large version.

00:36:14.550 --> 00:36:18.570
We've built this process of engineering
the gene pool of the company.

00:36:19.730 --> 00:36:23.390
And everybody got excited about changing
the course of their internet from

00:36:23.390 --> 00:36:28.110
what was ATM and nobody remembers this.

00:36:28.110 --> 00:36:34.017
A very, very senior executive told me
when I was looking at investing in this,

00:36:34.017 --> 00:36:36.351
they would never use TCP/IP.

00:36:36.351 --> 00:36:40.760
This is what telecom carriers do,
this is how traditional wisdom goes.

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:44.100
But you need this awesome
team to change that.

00:36:44.100 --> 00:36:52.371
And Pradeep Sindhu who was one of
the great founders I've run into.

00:36:52.371 --> 00:36:54.382
Not only built a great company, but

00:36:54.382 --> 00:36:59.020
it actually changed the trajectory of
the protocols of the Internet, in my view.

00:36:59.020 --> 00:37:01.990
We would have ended up
with something else.

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:05.030
Most likely ATM,
if all the carriers had their way.

00:37:06.340 --> 00:37:11.460
Every single carrier from
Deutsche Telekom in Europe to AT&amp;T here

00:37:11.460 --> 00:37:16.400
had decided they would go with ATM,
not TCP/IP.

00:37:16.400 --> 00:37:20.737
So, great missions,
great people to work with,

00:37:20.737 --> 00:37:24.572
all that is why you want
people in your team.

00:37:24.572 --> 00:37:27.950
&gt;&gt; Got it and
I want to touch upon functional hiring.

00:37:27.950 --> 00:37:31.660
Because that's, I think it is a component
of building a great team as well.

00:37:31.660 --> 00:37:37.020
And too often founders tend to prioritize
a lot on skills for a particular role

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:41.540
versus really figuring out the complete
package or the fit of the person.

00:37:42.790 --> 00:37:48.636
So can you talk about how should
a founder think about hiring?

00:37:48.636 --> 00:37:53.599
&gt;&gt; Let me answer that question but
later if your a founder,

00:37:53.599 --> 00:37:59.091
you just your technologists have
a technical break through or

00:37:59.091 --> 00:38:02.170
a big idea and you start a company.

00:38:03.530 --> 00:38:05.990
And you need a VPM Marketing and
you need a CFO.

00:38:05.990 --> 00:38:11.543
You should come back to the question, and
I covered this in my art of hiring of,

00:38:11.543 --> 00:38:15.771
how you judge somebody's good
VPM Marketing or a good CFO.

00:38:15.771 --> 00:38:19.340
Because the first thing you have to
realise is you're not qualified to judge.

00:38:21.030 --> 00:38:27.480
Knowing what you don't know is
critical and the most important

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:32.440
things that found that guys is decide
who's judgement to trust on, what topic.

00:38:34.450 --> 00:38:38.840
And, not just ask their friends
because they may not be qualified.

00:38:38.840 --> 00:38:43.558
In fact, hundreds has hired their friends
since gene pool engineering to start up.

00:38:43.558 --> 00:38:48.310
And that's wasting slots and talent too.

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:49.320
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:38:49.320 --> 00:38:51.680
&gt;&gt; But
let me go back to functional hiring.

00:38:51.680 --> 00:38:54.650
We can come back to this
otherwise I cover it fairly

00:38:54.650 --> 00:38:57.690
extensively in my paper on hiring.

00:38:57.690 --> 00:39:00.280
How do you interview people,
how do you judge them?

00:39:02.330 --> 00:39:04.772
If you need a VP of engineering,

00:39:04.772 --> 00:39:09.752
you can look at somebody who's
managed an engineering project.

00:39:09.752 --> 00:39:14.698
But if you're a founder,
specially an inexperienced founder with

00:39:14.698 --> 00:39:18.875
a big breakthrough idea in
a new space that nobody knows.

00:39:18.875 --> 00:39:23.182
You clearly need to build an engineering
came and execute on that, but

00:39:23.182 --> 00:39:24.720
you need more than that.

00:39:26.630 --> 00:39:31.250
Somebody who's going to go help
you evaluate all the pivots and

00:39:31.250 --> 00:39:32.970
changes and iterations in your plan.

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:37.850
Now let's say you hire a VP of
engineering who's good at that.

00:39:37.850 --> 00:39:42.020
A VP of marketing who's good at marketing,
a VP of sales who's good at sales, and

00:39:42.020 --> 00:39:44.550
a CFO and they do their job well.

00:39:45.580 --> 00:39:48.457
Who's going to help you
figure out what path to take?

00:39:51.503 --> 00:39:56.047
So I always say, you need functions,
but way more important,

00:39:56.047 --> 00:39:58.965
while hiring those functional people,

00:39:58.965 --> 00:40:03.880
you're getting something else,
whether you recognize it or not.

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:08.884
You getting somebody who's going to
participate in all the more critical

00:40:08.884 --> 00:40:09.580
decisions.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:15.594
Even more critical than having
engineering run more smoothly, which is,

00:40:15.594 --> 00:40:17.354
which path should we take?

00:40:17.354 --> 00:40:19.360
So ask the following question.

00:40:20.850 --> 00:40:25.200
When I hire a VP of engineering, how
will it make my VP of marketing better?

00:40:27.320 --> 00:40:29.970
That's why asking fundamental
first principles questions.

00:40:33.940 --> 00:40:37.970
Elon Musk's book is worth
reading because he knew so

00:40:37.970 --> 00:40:41.340
little about building cars or
about launching rockets.

00:40:42.660 --> 00:40:46.214
He made so many screwy mistakes
that could have been avoided.

00:40:48.867 --> 00:40:51.843
But if he had had all that experience and
knowledge,

00:40:51.843 --> 00:40:54.489
he wouldn't even have
attempted what he did.

00:40:54.489 --> 00:40:58.571
So, I'd rather have somebody
like Elon Musk who just

00:40:58.571 --> 00:41:01.750
fundamentally asks a lot of questions.

00:41:01.750 --> 00:41:03.280
So why are we doing it this way?

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:06.270
Not that we've done it this way for
50 years at GM.

00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:10.684
Remember, GM spent more money,
more time, and

00:41:10.684 --> 00:41:14.702
started earlier trying to do
an electric vehicle called the EV1.

00:41:17.011 --> 00:41:22.388
And Elon, with no experience,
by asking a lot of questions and

00:41:22.388 --> 00:41:27.257
adapting rapidly as he screwed up,
he got to a place where

00:41:27.257 --> 00:41:32.040
Tesla is a much more interesting
company than GM today.

00:41:33.650 --> 00:41:36.125
So when you look for that functional hire,

00:41:36.125 --> 00:41:39.927
realize you're also getting this other
thing which is much more important.

00:41:42.430 --> 00:41:47.821
Look for how they think, how fast they
change, how much they become defensive

00:41:47.821 --> 00:41:54.020
when you challenge an idea versus saying,
why didn't I think of that?

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:58.350
Or be much more brainstorm oriented.

00:41:59.778 --> 00:42:04.760
This sort of horizontal hiring that you
get while doing functional vertical

00:42:04.760 --> 00:42:09.530
hiring is much more important, and
a much more important dimension.

00:42:09.530 --> 00:42:12.055
And by the way,
most VCs don't look for it,

00:42:12.055 --> 00:42:16.027
because they've not gone through
building companies themselves.

00:42:16.027 --> 00:42:18.870
And so they give you bad advice.

00:42:20.570 --> 00:42:24.060
The number of times they say go get
somebody with distribution channel

00:42:24.060 --> 00:42:24.590
expertise.

00:42:26.340 --> 00:42:31.260
I'd rather have somebody figure it out and
not have a source of inertia in this

00:42:32.360 --> 00:42:36.190
fluid, dynamic, evolving,
thinking method in a company.

00:42:37.480 --> 00:42:42.300
So when you're hiring, more than
anything you're hiring a brain trust.

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:50.160
And they happen to also have skills in
doing functional jobs, but the brain trust

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:55.410
is more important than the functional job,
and evaluating them is very tricky.

00:42:57.390 --> 00:43:01.260
And it's tricky because partly you don't

00:43:01.260 --> 00:43:04.900
know how to evaluate somebody
outside of your area of competence.

00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:08.921
Do you call a VP of marketing at Cisco,

00:43:08.921 --> 00:43:14.017
I don't mean to pick on Cisco, but
it's a good company to pick on.

00:43:14.017 --> 00:43:18.014
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They will look at marketing very,

00:43:18.014 --> 00:43:21.960
very differently than
what a startup needs.

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:23.190
So they'll give you the wrong advice.

00:43:26.560 --> 00:43:30.470
Going through and
this is where the tricky part comes,

00:43:30.470 --> 00:43:35.060
not being overly swayed by titles or
position and understanding

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:40.000
why people did certain things and
how thoughtful were they about it.

00:43:41.290 --> 00:43:46.070
What did they do that
wasn't usual is very hard.

00:43:47.280 --> 00:43:52.840
It's also tricky to go back to this
question of how you evaluate people.

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:57.370
Most experienced interviewees know
how to give you the right answers.

00:43:59.060 --> 00:43:59.840
They're going to fool you.

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:04.775
You ask somebody okay,
hey what did you do here?

00:44:04.775 --> 00:44:09.140
They'll retroactively look at what
was great about the strategy and say,

00:44:09.140 --> 00:44:09.840
I did this.

00:44:11.610 --> 00:44:16.432
I've actually had people pitch me on

00:44:16.432 --> 00:44:21.572
why they did sans marketing strategy.

00:44:21.572 --> 00:44:26.377
Being very, very indirect,
kind of a list of questions

00:44:26.377 --> 00:44:30.890
in my paper on hiring,
the way you get to the bottom.

00:44:32.380 --> 00:44:35.070
So, I was, for example,
to give you one example,

00:44:35.070 --> 00:44:37.584
I'll ask the same thing
three different ways.

00:44:37.584 --> 00:44:42.139
I'll say at the beginning,
what do you love doing?

00:44:42.139 --> 00:44:43.712
What are you good at?

00:44:43.712 --> 00:44:45.610
Somebody'll say something.

00:44:45.610 --> 00:44:50.220
Then while talking later about a job,
I said what did you do really well there?

00:44:51.270 --> 00:44:56.190
If those two don't coincide, if
somebody's making it up, they're warned.

00:44:56.190 --> 00:44:58.270
I'll ask that question
three different ways.

00:44:59.960 --> 00:45:02.730
Third time, I might ask,
if you were going to join this company,

00:45:02.730 --> 00:45:05.560
what would you think
your strength would be?

00:45:05.560 --> 00:45:08.980
I've asked the same question three
different ways in three different

00:45:08.980 --> 00:45:12.630
contexts, people who are making it
up will make up different answers.

00:45:14.320 --> 00:45:18.059
People who fundamentally have the truth
will give the same, right answer.

00:45:19.240 --> 00:45:21.902
Then I might at the end
of the interview ask, so

00:45:21.902 --> 00:45:23.960
what would your boss say about you?

00:45:25.250 --> 00:45:28.040
Now I've asked the same
question a fourth time.

00:45:28.040 --> 00:45:30.070
If they say something then I'll say hey,

00:45:30.070 --> 00:45:32.750
can I get your last performance
review that your boss did?

00:45:34.230 --> 00:45:36.489
If they say no, I know they don't
want to share their performance.

00:45:36.489 --> 00:45:39.511
I have 100 tricks like this I use.

00:45:39.511 --> 00:45:45.068
You can never rely on the direct answer
you get in interviewing somebody.

00:45:45.068 --> 00:45:46.678
It's the trickiest thing.

00:45:46.678 --> 00:45:51.076
And then it's good to get
together with each other and

00:45:51.076 --> 00:45:53.724
see what each person looked at.

00:45:55.056 --> 00:46:00.061
One other danger in hiring,
I find like if I'm rushed today, and

00:46:00.061 --> 00:46:04.975
I'm interviewing somebody,
I'll evaluate them differently

00:46:04.975 --> 00:46:09.760
than if I'm doing it over
coffee on a Saturday morning.

00:46:09.760 --> 00:46:15.200
Other thing to keep in mind,
depending upon how your day went or

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:18.549
what mindset you are in,
how you evaluate people changes.

00:46:19.640 --> 00:46:24.010
So for critical positions, I'll
interview the same person three times at

00:46:24.010 --> 00:46:29.000
three different days, just so
I'm not biasing myself.

00:46:29.000 --> 00:46:33.780
Because humans, including yourself and
myself, are really biased.

00:46:33.780 --> 00:46:40.690
And so there's so many pitfalls,
this is why doing a startup is so hard.

00:46:41.960 --> 00:46:45.450
Anyway, I'll let you ask more questions.

00:46:45.450 --> 00:46:46.002
Sorry, I keep rambling.

00:46:46.002 --> 00:46:48.330
I could go on for six hours.

00:46:49.910 --> 00:46:53.560
&gt;&gt; I'll ask one more question and
then I'm going to open it up for Q and A.

00:46:53.560 --> 00:46:56.193
So, I know you talked, in that paper
you have a lot of questions and

00:46:56.193 --> 00:46:57.800
we will circulate that for interviews.

00:46:57.800 --> 00:46:59.854
But you also make a point
that in spite of,

00:46:59.854 --> 00:47:03.757
even if you follow all those processes,
you know, mistakes are inevitable.

00:47:03.757 --> 00:47:08.410
And at least one-third of the time,
the hires don't work out.

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:12.784
What in your view as like the most common
reason why the hires don't work out and

00:47:12.784 --> 00:47:14.706
how should a founder handle that,

00:47:14.706 --> 00:47:18.032
because sometimes start ups wait
too long to sort of fix it?

00:47:18.032 --> 00:47:24.183
&gt;&gt; Look, if somebody wants a job, in
their interview their job is to fool you.

00:47:24.183 --> 00:47:27.683
Question is are they
better at fooling you or

00:47:27.683 --> 00:47:31.190
are you better at getting
behind the words?

00:47:33.490 --> 00:47:37.792
I find after hiring many, many people, and

00:47:37.792 --> 00:47:42.689
I probably do hundreds of
interviews every year.

00:47:42.689 --> 00:47:46.495
I have a success rate of about 65%.

00:47:46.495 --> 00:47:51.766
Okay, I'm wrong and
I find most people with

00:47:51.766 --> 00:47:56.470
less experience are wrong more often.

00:47:56.470 --> 00:47:59.046
Because usually if you're a young founder,

00:47:59.046 --> 00:48:03.645
the people you are interviewing are more
experienced than you at fooling you.

00:48:03.645 --> 00:48:07.043
Okay, so it is a game.

00:48:10.330 --> 00:48:13.915
And we then talk about culture and
other things.

00:48:13.915 --> 00:48:18.264
There's those reasons why candidates fail,

00:48:18.264 --> 00:48:22.442
but really, not being able to get
behind the scenes and dig deep.

00:48:22.442 --> 00:48:23.119
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:48:27.130 --> 00:48:30.850
&gt;&gt; So one,
you ought to plan on that happening.

00:48:30.850 --> 00:48:35.604
I always tell my teams,
if you want four people on your team,

00:48:35.604 --> 00:48:39.344
hire six,
even make up a title you don't need.

00:48:39.344 --> 00:48:44.102
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I always overhire,

00:48:44.102 --> 00:48:50.280
because you're never successful,
and hiring is a long process.

00:48:50.280 --> 00:48:57.031
To hire a critical person in a startup,
whether it's a critical technologist or

00:48:57.031 --> 00:49:02.010
a senior executive,
can easily take six months or longer.

00:49:03.880 --> 00:49:06.670
And then they take six
months coming up to speed,

00:49:06.670 --> 00:49:11.575
so if you've made a mistake,
you lose a year.

00:49:11.575 --> 00:49:17.260
And they will have hired other
crappy people during that time.

00:49:17.260 --> 00:49:19.780
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And so it's very, very expensive.

00:49:21.392 --> 00:49:25.559
This is why you really need
to watch new people, so

00:49:25.559 --> 00:49:31.500
you need to really evaluate
how they're doing.

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:35.251
And this is where I think both members and

00:49:35.251 --> 00:49:40.669
investors should be much more
about assisting a venture.

00:49:40.669 --> 00:49:47.389
So I never call myself a venture
capitalist, I say a venture assistant, and

00:49:47.389 --> 00:49:53.360
so even our tagline for
our firm says venture assistant.

00:49:53.360 --> 00:49:58.360
I like to do all this, and
spend all my time doing all this.

00:49:58.360 --> 00:50:03.590
I never go to VC functions,
I never go to financial conferences,

00:50:03.590 --> 00:50:08.160
I just spend my time with founders and
people they're trying to hire.

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:12.298
And by the way, it's a lot more fun,
and you learn a lot more, and

00:50:12.298 --> 00:50:15.393
get excited about it,
but you should overhire.

00:50:15.393 --> 00:50:16.630
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:50:16.630 --> 00:50:20.440
&gt;&gt; Flip side, if you find somebody great,
make up a title and hire.

00:50:20.440 --> 00:50:23.310
Two things,

00:50:23.310 --> 00:50:26.670
no great talent is worth getting
away because you don't have a slot.

00:50:28.460 --> 00:50:32.400
Make up a slot,
they will pay for themselves.

00:50:32.400 --> 00:50:38.055
The flip side, with few exceptions,
and there are some,

00:50:38.055 --> 00:50:45.415
a great talent is never
too expensive to hire.

00:50:45.415 --> 00:50:52.920
Okay, so I've gone through all
kinds of ridiculous things.

00:50:52.920 --> 00:50:55.987
We were trying to hire an exec today, and

00:50:55.987 --> 00:50:59.338
I was arguing with
another VC this morning.

00:50:59.338 --> 00:51:03.880
I said, so his company offered him this,
they're about to go public.

00:51:03.880 --> 00:51:09.030
Why don't we guarantee him the first
$15 million out of this startup,

00:51:09.030 --> 00:51:11.215
give him a preference
over the preferred stock?

00:51:11.215 --> 00:51:16.814
I'll go to any length for great talent.

00:51:16.814 --> 00:51:20.913
Nobody heard of putting 15 million
of preference, for an employee,

00:51:20.913 --> 00:51:24.305
above the preferred stock,
with the investors' money.

00:51:24.305 --> 00:51:27.269
Well, I've done this more than once.

00:51:27.269 --> 00:51:27.889
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:51:31.022 --> 00:51:35.848
&gt;&gt; So a couple of things around that.

00:51:35.848 --> 00:51:41.616
The flip side is plan for
failure and evaluate new people.

00:51:41.616 --> 00:51:45.703
One other thing, also, when you
hire somebody, and I want to stop.

00:51:48.679 --> 00:51:51.670
Try and assimilate them.

00:51:51.670 --> 00:51:55.094
People can fail because
they're the wrong fit,

00:51:55.094 --> 00:51:59.869
but people can also fail because
you didn't assimilate them well.

00:51:59.869 --> 00:52:03.269
See, if you have someone really
experienced in a business,

00:52:03.269 --> 00:52:07.937
and you hired somebody from that business,
they can advise you on how to assimilate

00:52:07.937 --> 00:52:12.071
this person, because you might think
of what they're doing differently than

00:52:12.071 --> 00:52:14.890
somebody who knows that person or
that business.

00:52:15.910 --> 00:52:19.440
So getting advice on how to
assimilate people into your business,

00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:22.000
because they are going to be different.

00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:25.810
They are the diversity you're trying to
be, and you don't want to be too uniform.

00:52:27.080 --> 00:52:31.965
So getting help in assimilating,
getting lots of advice

00:52:31.965 --> 00:52:37.040
on how to make them successful, as opposed
to reacting because they're acting so

00:52:37.040 --> 00:52:39.800
differently and
doing things so differently.

00:52:39.800 --> 00:52:45.183
That's bad, too, but then, of course, if
you make a mistake you have to be quick.

00:52:45.183 --> 00:52:45.707
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:52:45.707 --> 00:52:49.372
&gt;&gt; And
good investors can help you do that.

00:52:49.372 --> 00:52:50.352
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:52:50.352 --> 00:52:53.160
&gt;&gt; That, to me, is the principle
value good investors bring.

00:52:54.365 --> 00:52:58.170
Anyway, let's open it up to questions for
a few minutes.

00:52:58.170 --> 00:53:01.706
&gt;&gt; I have a question, so
you talk about going up,

00:53:01.706 --> 00:53:07.796
I'm going to ask about the space rocket,
and Tesla, and then Theranos.

00:53:07.796 --> 00:53:13.055
Both of them, they are college dropouts,
but why do you think one of them,

00:53:13.055 --> 00:53:17.830
it requires good domain expertise,
and then one of them doesn't?

00:53:17.830 --> 00:53:22.403
Well, different questions, one,

00:53:22.403 --> 00:53:27.866
I think Stripe hired really awesome teams.

00:53:27.866 --> 00:53:32.925
In fact, the Stripe co-founder
that is now is now running OpenAI,

00:53:32.925 --> 00:53:38.500
he hired somebody so strong,
even though he was a college dropout.

00:53:38.500 --> 00:53:43.340
Patrick, right,
he hired awesome people around him.

00:53:44.620 --> 00:53:46.550
I don't think Theranos did this early.

00:53:48.920 --> 00:53:53.056
There's a culture thing we didn't talk
about, and culture is very important.

00:53:53.056 --> 00:53:58.550
I think good companies

00:53:58.550 --> 00:54:01.800
acknowledge their problems, say,
what do we need to solve it?

00:54:01.800 --> 00:54:04.460
And Theranos, early on,
tried to hide their problems.

00:54:05.940 --> 00:54:08.661
They didn't encourage discussion
around their problems.

00:54:12.874 --> 00:54:14.263
And I think that was the difference.

00:54:14.263 --> 00:54:16.750
I think Elizabeth has learned a lot.

00:54:16.750 --> 00:54:19.980
I think if she was to do another startup,
she'd do much, much better.

00:54:19.980 --> 00:54:26.840
But sometimes, in trying to show
the outside world you're a great startup,

00:54:26.840 --> 00:54:30.940
you tend to not acknowledge your
weaknesses, and that's a danger.

00:54:32.340 --> 00:54:40.425
In fact, more startups spend way more time
on their assets, not their liabilities.

00:54:40.425 --> 00:54:45.186
Your liabilities can kill
you faster than your assets

00:54:45.186 --> 00:54:49.856
can make you successful,
so you need to spend time.

00:54:49.856 --> 00:54:55.664
Because you're a cohesive group,
generally, when you're starting out,

00:54:55.664 --> 00:55:00.859
you tend to have groupthink, so
you tend to lose sight of your risks.

00:55:04.569 --> 00:55:09.589
And you want to encourage that behavior
of asking a lot, that's culture.

00:55:09.589 --> 00:55:15.595
We won't go too much into culture today,
but establishing the right values,

00:55:15.595 --> 00:55:21.350
why people are here, what the mission is,
what kind of culture.

00:55:21.350 --> 00:55:26.760
And a number of people made the point,
having goodies in your lunch menu isn't

00:55:26.760 --> 00:55:33.760
culture, allowing dogs in your workplace
isn't culture, though I love dogs.

00:55:34.890 --> 00:55:38.618
Culture is about fundamental values, and
how you behave, and what you encourage.

00:55:38.618 --> 00:55:43.623
And the more people,
at every level, in the organization

00:55:43.623 --> 00:55:48.730
who can ask questions, the more you
will identify your risks sooner,

00:55:48.730 --> 00:55:54.530
have a more constructive culture, and
the more likely you will increase success.

00:55:54.530 --> 00:55:56.543
Let me finish that by
saying one other thing.

00:56:01.473 --> 00:56:04.099
Sam said something else
that I really liked,

00:56:04.099 --> 00:56:07.210
he said startups are a pass/fail game.

00:56:07.210 --> 00:56:09.530
Then put everything I've
been saying in context.

00:56:10.700 --> 00:56:16.010
I like to say startups not about dilution.

00:56:16.010 --> 00:56:18.710
They're about the probability of success.

00:56:18.710 --> 00:56:20.670
They're much more distill than you think.

00:56:22.210 --> 00:56:25.990
If you can increase the probability
of success by hiring great people.

00:56:27.630 --> 00:56:30.247
By asking a lot of questions,

00:56:30.247 --> 00:56:35.916
by finding your risks early by
having people surface problems.

00:56:35.916 --> 00:56:38.130
You're going to increase
your probability of success.

00:56:39.310 --> 00:56:45.680
And either you're successful or
you're not and so it's about probability.

00:56:45.680 --> 00:56:48.330
And people worry way too
much about dilution and

00:56:49.610 --> 00:56:53.110
not giving more equity to a co-founder or

00:56:53.110 --> 00:56:58.170
a new hire who may need
to be made a co-founder.

00:56:58.170 --> 00:57:00.780
I'm seeing a lot of this
in the AI startups now.

00:57:02.890 --> 00:57:08.455
They're AI startups, but they don't
hire the AI talent because, hey,

00:57:08.455 --> 00:57:13.752
the average AI person at Google,
machine learning person is making,

00:57:13.752 --> 00:57:17.819
the median income I
think is like $750,000.

00:57:17.819 --> 00:57:19.054
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:57:19.054 --> 00:57:20.805
&gt;&gt; What you going to
do to get that person.

00:57:20.805 --> 00:57:21.460
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:57:21.460 --> 00:57:23.370
&gt;&gt; You can pretend you
can do without that.

00:57:24.760 --> 00:57:26.580
But you're fooling yourself.

00:57:26.580 --> 00:57:27.454
Sorry, there was a question there.

00:57:27.454 --> 00:57:28.728
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:57:28.728 --> 00:57:30.429
&gt;&gt; You touched on this earlier, and

00:57:30.429 --> 00:57:32.990
you spend your Saturdays
going through resumes.

00:57:32.990 --> 00:57:37.410
So for early stage, could you go
through the 30 second to one minute,

00:57:37.410 --> 00:57:39.270
where you look for, if you're hiring,

00:57:39.270 --> 00:57:44.880
let's say, probably technical or
early business [INAUDIBLE] person.

00:57:44.880 --> 00:57:45.975
&gt;&gt; What do I look for?

00:57:45.975 --> 00:57:46.890
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:57:46.890 --> 00:57:54.611
&gt;&gt; First, people who can think reasonably
about their area, whatever their area is.

00:57:54.611 --> 00:58:00.894
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
Is it a [INAUDIBLE] LinkedIn?

00:58:00.894 --> 00:58:03.570
&gt;&gt; Sometimes just if I ask

00:58:03.570 --> 00:58:07.060
somebody what interesting papers have
you read in your area of expertise.

00:58:08.390 --> 00:58:11.173
If it's narrow diversity,
it tells me one thing,

00:58:11.173 --> 00:58:14.420
if it's broad diversity it's
one of my 100 questions.

00:58:14.420 --> 00:58:18.218
I'll ask this first questions
like what books have you read.

00:58:18.218 --> 00:58:20.570
But it tells you how they think.

00:58:21.730 --> 00:58:29.430
And that's critical to a start-up in
addition to knowing encryption algorithms.

00:58:29.430 --> 00:58:30.610
Ann, you tell me when to stop.

00:58:30.610 --> 00:58:31.190
I'll keep asking.

00:58:31.190 --> 00:58:33.247
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I think we're probably at the last one.

00:58:33.247 --> 00:58:34.230
&gt;&gt; Great.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:58:34.230 --> 00:58:37.670
&gt;&gt; So, for an early stage start-up
that's bootstrapping do you see

00:58:37.670 --> 00:58:41.588
any value in leveraging technical
advisors or an advisory board?

00:58:41.588 --> 00:58:46.013
&gt;&gt; There is always value
in leveraging technical

00:58:46.013 --> 00:58:49.420
advisors in their advisory board.

00:58:50.820 --> 00:58:55.471
What is important to get
to get their opinion, but

00:58:55.471 --> 00:59:00.807
not respect it too much,
this is a quandary that is hard.

00:59:00.807 --> 00:59:06.860
You want them to surface the issues, but
you as the founder using first principles.

00:59:07.900 --> 00:59:10.300
One will say,
do I believe there's a knot or

00:59:10.300 --> 00:59:12.950
how much should I weigh this
versus some other things.

00:59:14.070 --> 00:59:16.340
That's the critical tought,
that's the hard part.

00:59:17.610 --> 00:59:20.620
That's what makes entrepreneurship hard,

00:59:20.620 --> 00:59:25.800
because it is a lot of fun but
it's really hard gift.

00:59:25.800 --> 00:59:30.900
I have a great presentation,
I came up with the title in 1986 and

00:59:30.900 --> 00:59:32.050
still in our website,

00:59:32.050 --> 00:59:36.800
the entrepreneurial roller coaster with
the high is a high and the low is a low.

00:59:38.310 --> 00:59:42.443
And the low's a really
low in every start off.

00:59:42.443 --> 00:59:46.518
&gt;&gt; Yes that's true, we'll thank you so
much for taking the time.

00:59:46.518 --> 00:59:47.484
&gt;&gt; Thank you everybody.

00:59:47.484 --> 00:59:48.339
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

